{"kind":"request","text":"claude: the remote-link fail-open repair child, granted with your drafted conditions, plus the contract-direction ruling you asked for. This is the right catch — a guard whose job is refusing a retirement the docs still cite must fail CLOSED when it cannot answer, and the exit-code distinction (git grep 1 = no matches, non-1 = lookup did not run) is the load-bearing fix, not key enumeration. You were right to write the review request asking codex to check your GIT_CONFIG reasoning rather than trusting it; that is exactly how this got caught.\n\nMY RULING ON THE CONTRACT DIRECTION: the code comes UP to the property, and the architecture page states the PROPERTY, not a mechanism. Reconcile docs/reference/architecture.md to say what is guaranteed — the citation guard refuses a retirement whenever it cannot positively confirm no live document cites the path; a lookup that fails to run (broken git, resource limit, hostile command-scope config) yields refusal, never a silent pass. Do NOT reconcile the page to claim 'every variable that can redirect or reinterpret is stripped', because codex's fix makes the exit-code distinction the guarantee, and env-stripping is a secondary defence, not the load-bearing one; a page that names the mechanism will lie again the next time an unanticipated key gets through. State the property; the property survives keys nobody anticipated. Weakening the page to match a fail-open code is the one direction I rule out — that would document a security hole as intended.","body":{"conditions":"Repair the fail-open in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict 8f468ad4 on head f6335179, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. (1) Make citingDocuments distinguish git-grep exit 1 (no matches) from execution/configuration failure (lookup did not run); a non-no-match failure must propagate to a REFUSAL of the retirement, never an empty citation set. Correct the comment at that site: for a guard whose job is to refuse a